course: history
lesson: 48
title: Chornobyl and independence in 1991
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the 1960s young poets and artists, the «Sixtiers», wrote in Ukrainian about truth and freedom; the Soviet government feared words that make people think, and many, like the poet Vasyl Stus, were jailed.
explain: On 26 April 1986 reactor 4 at the Chornobyl nuclear plant exploded; the authorities kept quiet for days, the May Day parade in Kyiv went ahead, and Sweden noticed the radiation first, like a neighbour smelling smoke before the family next door admits the kitchen is on fire.
explain: Danylo said Chornobyl «killed millions», and the teacher said «only 31 died»; both were wrong in opposite ways: about 31 died in the first months, while later deaths from radiation are still studied and estimates range from thousands to tens of thousands.
explain: On 24 August 1991 the Ukrainian parliament declared independence, and on 1 December over 90% of voters said yes, with majorities in every region, Crimea included; Danylo thought there had been a war, but independence was won peacefully, by vote.
explain: The new country chose old symbols, the trident and the hryvnia, and gave up the nuclear weapons left on its soil for promises in the Budapest Memorandum; the 1990s were hard, and the wise thing is to ask parents and grandparents about the history they lived.
fact: In the 1960s the «Sixtiers», such as Lina Kostenko, Vasyl Symonenko and Ivan Dziuba, defended the Ukrainian language and free thought; many were later jailed, and Vasyl Stus died in a Soviet camp in 1985.
fact: On 26 April 1986 reactor 4 of the Chornobyl nuclear power plant exploded; about 31 people died in the first months, tens of thousands were evacuated for good, and later deaths from radiation (invisible harmful rays) are debated, because cancers can appear years later and also have other causes, with estimates from thousands to tens of thousands.
fact: Soviet authorities first kept quiet about Chornobyl; Sweden detected the radiation on 28 April, Moscow announced it only briefly that evening, and the May Day parade in Kyiv went ahead on 1 May.
fact: Chornobyl is the name of a kind of mugwort, a wormwood plant; some people later linked it to the «star Wormwood» in the Book of Revelation, a coincidence noticed after the disaster.
fact: Wildlife such as wolves and Przewalski's horses returned to the Chornobyl exclusion zone (the closed area around the plant), and before the 2022 war guided visits were possible; a New Safe Confinement, a huge arched shelter, was placed over reactor 4 in 2016, Russian troops occupied the area in 2022, and a drone damaged the confinement in 2025, as was reported.
fact: The Ukrainian parliament declared independence on 24 August 1991, after a failed coup (an attempt to seize power) in Moscow; in the referendum of 1 December 1991 over 90% voted for independence, with majorities in every region, including Crimea.
fact: In 1991 Ukraine had the world's third-largest stock of nuclear weapons on its soil, though Moscow held the launch controls; under the 1994 Budapest Memorandum it gave them up for assurances from the USA, the UK and Russia to respect its borders, which Russia later broke.
fact: The hryvnia was a silver unit in Kyivan Rus; the modern hryvnia, Ukraine's money, was introduced in 1996.
fact: The tryzub (trident) appears on coins of Volodymyr the Great; it became the state emblem in 1918 and again in 1992, and its original meaning is debated.
fact: The blue-and-yellow flag comes from Galician coats of arms and was used by Ukrainians in 1848; it was used in 1918 and adopted again in 1992, and the «sky over wheat» reading is popular and later.
fact: The 1990s in independent Ukraine brought hyperinflation, when prices rise extremely fast, closed factories, poverty and corruption, as in many post-Soviet states; growth came in the 2000s.
exercise: Who were the «Sixtiers»? | Young writers and artists of the 1960s who defended language and free thought | Cossacks of the 1600s | Soviet rocket builders who sent the first satellites and people into space in the 1960s | Footballers of the 1960s
exercise: Which poet died in a Soviet camp in 1985? | Vasyl Stus | Taras Shevchenko | Ivan Franko | Lesia Ukrainka
exercise: Why were poets dangerous to the Soviet government? | Their words made people think freely and ask questions | They carried weapons and planned to take over the Kremlin by force | They controlled the army | They stole the grain
exercise: When did the Chornobyl reactor explode? | 26 April 1986 | 24 August 1991 | 6 November 1943 | 1 December 1991
exercise: About how many people died in the first months after Chornobyl? | About 31 | About 3 million | Nobody at all | About 300,000
exercise: Why is it hard to count deaths from radiation? | Cancers appear years later and also have other causes | Radiation kills nobody ever, so there is nothing to count | All records were burnt in 1986 | Doctors are not allowed to count
exercise: Which country first detected the Chornobyl radiation? | Sweden | The United States | Japan, far to the east | Australia
exercise: What went ahead in Kyiv on 1 May 1986 despite the danger? | The May Day parade | The Eurovision final | The Olympic Games | A Cossack council
exercise: What does the name Chornobyl mean? | A kind of mugwort, a wormwood plant | Black ice on the river in winter | Big river | The iron star from the Bible
exercise: Which animals returned to the Chornobyl zone? | Wolves and Przewalski's horses | Lions, giraffes and zebras from Africa | Penguins and seals | Kangaroos and koalas
exercise: What was placed over reactor 4 in 2016? | A New Safe Confinement | A tall glass pyramid like the Louvre's | A football stadium | A giant pysanka
exercise: When did the Ukrainian parliament declare independence? | 24 August 1991 | 22 January 1918 | 1 December 1991 | 24 February 2022
exercise: What share of voters supported independence on 1 December 1991? | Over 90% | About 30% | Exactly half | Under 10%
exercise: What did Ukraine give up under the 1994 Budapest Memorandum? | The nuclear weapons on its soil | Crimea | Its flag and its trident, and its own army | Its seat at the UN
exercise: What did Russia promise in the Budapest Memorandum? | To respect Ukraine's borders | To build Ukraine a new reactor | To pay for the hryvnia | To join the EU
exercise: When was the modern hryvnia introduced? | 1996 | 1240 | 1854 | 2014
exercise: Where did the trident appear in Volodymyr's time? | On his coins | On the Moon's surface | On Cossack boats | On the Eiffel Tower
exercise: Where do the colours of the blue-and-yellow flag come from? | Galician coats of arms, used in 1848 | A Cossack flag that Khmelnytsky flew in 1648 | A Eurovision costume | A Swedish football club
test: Danylo says Ukrainian poets beat the USSR with poems. What's fairer? | They didn't bring it down alone, but they kept the culture alive | Yes, one poem toppled the USSR | Poets had nothing to do with anything that happened in the Soviet Union | Only Russian poets wrote then
test: Danylo says Chornobyl killed millions; teacher says only 31. Who's right? | Neither: 31 died quickly, and long-term deaths are debated | Danylo, millions | The teacher, and nobody else ever died of it, not even years later | Both, it killed exactly 31 million
test: «moscow hid chornobyl from literally everyone» — is that right? | Mostly: they announced it briefly, but hid the danger for days | Totally wrong, they warned everyone at once on the radio and on television | Sweden caused the accident | Nobody ever found out
test: If the authorities had warned Kyiv at once, what might have happened? | Fewer people would have been outside at the parade | The reactor would not have exploded | Nothing could have been different | Sweden would have closed
test: Danylo says the Bible predicted Chornobyl because of the name. Wise reply? | It's an interesting coincidence, not a prediction | It's a proven prophecy that scientists have checked | The name was invented in 1986 | The Bible mentions Kyiv's metro
test: «Nobody can ever go near Chornobyl again, it's dead forever». fix it. | Guided visits were possible before the war, and wildlife came back | Correct, no living thing is there, not even grass, insects or birds | People live there normally in big cities | It's a holiday beach now
test: Danylo says Ukraine fought a war to win independence in 1991. What's true? | It was won peacefully, by parliament's vote and a referendum | It took a long ten-year war against the Soviet army in the mountains | Russia's army handed it over in a battle | Ukraine has never been independent
test: Danylo says Crimea voted no in 1991. teacher shows the map. What's on it? | A majority in every region voted yes, Crimea included | Only Kyiv voted yes | Crimea didn't vote at all | Everyone voted no
test: «Ukraine had the 3rd-biggest nuclear army and could nuke anyone». What's more exact? | The weapons were on its soil, but Moscow held the launch controls | Ukraine built them all itself in secret factories near Dnipro and Kharkiv | Ukraine never had any | It used them in 1993
test: Danylo says the hryvnia is the oldest money in the world. Fix it. | The name is from Kyivan Rus, the money is from 1996, and older coins exist | Yes, the first coin ever | It was invented in 2014 | The hryvnia is a Swedish coin
test: Danylo says Cossacks chose blue for sky and yellow for wheat. What did they find? | The colours come from Galician arms of 1848; the sky-wheat idea came later | Exactly what the Cossacks decreed | The flag was red and black | The flag was chosen by Eurovision
test: Danylo says after 1991 Ukraine got rich at once. his dad laughs. Why? | The 1990s brought hyperinflation, closed factories and poverty | Everyone got a free car | Ukraine joined the EU in 1992 | Prices never changed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the accession of Ukraine to independence in nineteen hundred and ninety-one? | It was declared by parliament and confirmed by a referendum in which over ninety per cent voted in favour | It was achieved through a prolonged war against the Soviet army lasting nearly a decade and costing many lives | It was conferred by the United Nations without a vote | It was rejected by a majority of voters in every region
exam: Identify the undertaking given to Ukraine pursuant to the Budapest Memorandum of nineteen hundred and ninety-four in exchange for the relinquishment of nuclear weapons. | Assurances by the United States, the United Kingdom and Russia to respect its borders | Immediate membership of the European Union | The transfer of Crimea to Turkey | The construction of a new nuclear arsenal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the immediate fatalities resulting from the Chornobyl accident of nineteen hundred and eighty-six. | Approximately thirty-one persons died within the first months, while long-term fatalities remain debated | Several million persons died within the first week | No person sustained any harm whatsoever | Precisely one hundred thousand persons died upon the first day, and none thereafter from any illness
exam: Ascertain the origin of the state emblem of Ukraine known as the tryzub. | It appears upon coins of Volodymyr the Great | It was designed for the Eurovision contest | It was introduced by the Ems decree | It was copied from the flag of Sweden
